What does the top command show?

It’s like Task Manager in Windows — but in the Linux terminal.

You can see:
	•	Which apps are running in the background
	•	Who is using too much power (CPU/RAM)
	•	And if you want, you can stop a problem app from there

“top is like checking who in your house is doing the most nakhre and wasting all the resources! 

You run top and Linux says:
👉 ‘This guy is eating all the CPU,
👉 that one is sleeping,
👉 and this one is doing drama with memory!’

So basically, it shows you:
Who’s working, who’s chilling, and who’s killing your server!

Basically, it’s the Bigg Boss of Linux — showing you who’s performing, who’s lazy, and who needs to be kicked out
